WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.769
-Our next guest
is an Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:04.971
you know from TV shows such as
"Unbreakable Kimmy Schmidt"

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:06.439
and "The Office"
and films like

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.274
"Bridesmaids"
and "The Stand In."

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:10.776
She stars
in "Home Sweet Home Alone,"

00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:12.345
which is now streaming
on Disney+.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:13.446
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:15.248
-So, did you get my voicemail?
-Yes.

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:16.882
All of this happened
in the last two hours?

00:00:16.882 --> 00:00:18.384
-Yeah, the doll
is in that house.

00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:19.518
I saw him in his jacket.

00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:20.720
-Why didn't you did tell me
any of this?

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.021
-I thought
I would handle it myself.

00:00:22.021 --> 00:00:24.623
-Your way of handling it
was breaking-and-entering?

00:00:24.623 --> 00:00:26.192
-Just entering.
I had the key.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.593
-It's still against the law,
Jeff.

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:29.529
-Like a light bulb!
-Oh, my gosh.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:32.999
This is so terrible.
-♪ All of the other reindeer ♪

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:34.367
-That's why I didn't do it,
Columbo.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.635
-Well, good.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:37.136
-♪ Like Pinocchio ♪
-♪ Pinocchio ♪

00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:38.237
-What are we gonna do,
though, Pam?

00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:39.872
We need that doll.
-I know we do, okay?

00:00:39.872 --> 00:00:43.943
Just let me think.
-♪ Join in any reindeer games ♪

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:46.279
-Um...
-♪ Like "Monopoly" ♪

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:47.647
That's too late.

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:49.148
-Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:49.148 --> 00:00:50.950
the always wonderful
Ellie Kemper!

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.952
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:52.952 --> 00:01:02.962 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:05.030 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.300
-You look a dream!
-Thank you!

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.501
This is the first time
I've been out in months.

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:12.004
I feel -- I feel --
It's nice to put on high heels.

00:01:12.004 --> 00:01:13.706
-Well, you look wonderful.

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:16.242
You know, I used to not
have to go out to see you.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:18.277
We used to live in the same
building in New York City.

00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:20.313
-Yep.
-You had some very

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:23.249
hurtful opinions about my dog.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:24.684
-If you could call it a dog,
Seth.

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:26.885
-Well, see, that's --
-No, no. Out of the gate.

00:01:26.885 --> 00:01:28.221
-That's the heart
of the hurtful.

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.322
[ Laughter ]
-I know.

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:30.723
-Now, I've always
written that off

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:32.725
as you not being a dog person.
-Yep.

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:34.660
-And so then,
I was happy to hear,

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.328
because I feel like
it is fair punishment...

00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:37.730
-Yep.
-...that your boys

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.566
are dog people.
-Very much.

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:42.235
I have two boys, 5 and 2,
and they love dogs.

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:44.270
We do not have a dog.
They love dogs.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.205
I have the nicest neighbors
in the world.

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:48.741
They have the cutest --
a very cute dog named Benny.

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:52.478
-That's even more hurtful to me
that now you're making it clear

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:54.747
you can perceive cuteness
in a dog.

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:57.917
-I am capable of love for dogs
when they pass the grade.

00:01:57.917 --> 00:01:59.285
-Deserve it.
Yeah, yeah, yeah, sure.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.887
-But this dog --
but I have to tell you --

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:02.555
And we're in Central Park
all the time.

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:04.256
They love, love dogs.
I'm very straightforward

00:02:04.256 --> 00:02:06.893
with them -- I'm not going
to have a dog only because

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:09.895
it's a huge responsibility,
as you know better than anyone.

00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:13.332
Being the dog, you know,
the owner that you are.

00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:14.633
I couldn't think
of another word.

00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:17.203
I said, "Yeah, the dog owner."
And so, I'm like --

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:19.038
I'm not even
sugarcoating it for them.

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:21.507
I'm very direct with them.
"I'm sorry, boys."

00:02:21.507 --> 00:02:23.876
Like, "You will not have a dog
in your childhood."

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:25.544
It's killer! It's so mean.
-Yeah.

00:02:25.544 --> 00:02:27.446
-But I don't want to raise
their hopes only to dash them.

00:02:27.446 --> 00:02:28.781
-Right.
-So I'm just, you know...

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:30.617
-But do you feel like
they've accepted this reality?

00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:32.252
-Yeah, well, for Christmas.

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:33.652
I think they think a dog
might come for Christmas.

00:02:33.652 --> 00:02:34.987
-Okay, gotcha.
-So, no, not completely.

00:02:34.987 --> 00:02:38.357
-So, by January, they should be,
like, really buying into

00:02:38.357 --> 00:02:40.759
what you've been telling them.
-Exactly, when there is no dog.

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:42.094
-When there's no dog.
-Under the tree.

00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:43.395
I'm like, "I wasn't lying."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.395 --> 00:02:46.632
-So you did --
You shot this in Canada.

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:48.668
-Yes.
-And your husband,

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:49.969
who's an incredibly
talented writer.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:51.671
-Yeah.
-The two of you had a bit

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:54.073
of help with the kids,
which is necessary.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:55.140
-Yes.
-You had a nanny.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.609
-We had a nanny.
She was wonderful.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.577
Her name was Beth.
She was from London.

00:02:58.577 --> 00:03:00.046
She had a little bit
of Mary Poppins in her.

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:01.347
-Oh, that's good.
-Very good.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:03.549
She's a pro.
Like, I owe my life to her.

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:05.217
She took such great care
of our kids.

00:03:05.217 --> 00:03:07.786
One thing -- this is sort of a
page from my book about dogs --

00:03:07.786 --> 00:03:11.558
she was very direct with the
boys about life and coronavirus.

00:03:11.558 --> 00:03:14.259
And I had told Beth, I said,
"You know, the boys -- James,"

00:03:14.259 --> 00:03:16.762
who's the older ones,
he knows what coronavirus is."

00:03:16.762 --> 00:03:18.464
You know, I've told them
it's a cold,

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:20.299
and that we want to be careful
with our germs.

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:21.467
And she was like,
"Right, right."

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:22.835
I said, "But I don't want
to scare him."

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:25.371
And so, I came home, like,
from work one day,

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:28.908
and James was talking about RNA,
and I was like --

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:31.177
She has a background -- She has,
like, a medical background.

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:32.545
Part of her training was,
you know,

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:35.280
attending some sort of
medical school.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.549
And she was so direct.

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:38.785
She's like,
"It's not a cold, James."

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:40.152
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:41.920
"It's much more contagious
than a cold."

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.722
And then whatever
RNA plays into,

00:03:43.722 --> 00:03:45.424
she gave them the information
on that.

00:03:45.424 --> 00:03:48.827
And she absolutely, like,
dealt them the straight story.

00:03:48.827 --> 00:03:51.431
He was also very interested
in Greek myths at the time,

00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:52.798
and I said,
"We kind of gloss over, like,

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:54.200
the more horrific parts of --"
You know what I mean?

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.868
-Yeah, because it gets
pretty gnarly.

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:57.670
-It's very gnarly, very violent.
-Yeah.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.038
-People killing each other
because they slept

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:00.440
with their their mother,
you know, all this stuff.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.107
She's like --
And, again, James came home

00:04:02.107 --> 00:04:03.409
one day -- or,
I came home one day,

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:05.677
and he's talking about --
I don't know my Greek myths.

00:04:05.677 --> 00:04:08.113
But he's like, "Well, you know,
Zeus, like --"

00:04:08.113 --> 00:04:09.749
I was gonna say something
really horrible.

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:12.918
"Zeus, like, killed,
you know, his mother."

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:15.954
I don't know Greek myths.
And I said, "Oh, Beth, you know,

00:04:15.954 --> 00:04:19.725
we were glossing over the sort
of, like, more horrible parts."

00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:21.660
She's like, "Oh, no.
Why would you do that?"

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.929
It was like, she was so --

00:04:23.929 --> 00:04:27.333
I appreciated, like,
that tact, I guess,

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.234
but they know everything
about everything now.

00:04:29.234 --> 00:04:32.271
-I do like that she stood up
for, like, the Greek myths.

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:34.106
-Exactly!
-They have survived

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:35.908
oral tradition for years.

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:38.411
We're not gonna just let you
change them at the last minute.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:40.313
-Thank you, yeah!
-Also, that's a huge part

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:42.014
of who Zeus is.
-I know.

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:43.215
He kills people!
-He kills people.

00:04:43.215 --> 00:04:44.784
-He sleeps with their mothers
and kills people.

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:47.286
-You can't sugarcoat Zeus.
-I know. I know.

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:51.690
No, she was right.
-Do you remember seeing

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.092
"Home Alone"
when it came out in 1990?

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:54.460
-Yes, I loved that.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.963
I mean,
it's, like, an iconic movie.

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:59.165
And when we went to see it --
I lived in St. Louis --

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:00.766
we went, I think,
the day after Thanksgiving.

00:05:00.766 --> 00:05:04.036
And my Aunt Dot --
that was her name, Aunt Dot.

00:05:04.036 --> 00:05:07.573
She was in the car with us.
She was my mom's aunt.

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:09.208
And we went to
the Saint Louis Galleria.

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:11.310
We found a parking spot so close
to the actual mall,

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.514
my Aunt Dot said,
"Well, that is the work of God

00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:15.847
that we found --"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:15.847 --> 00:05:18.217
And even at 8 years --
God does a lot of good things.

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:21.120
I knew he didn't have that much
control over parking spots

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.589
even at that age.
But it was, like, a sign

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:24.856
of the good things
that were to come.

00:05:24.856 --> 00:05:26.225
Because that movie, like,

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:27.993
it was a packed audience
laughing out loud.

00:05:27.993 --> 00:05:29.728
Like, it's one of the most
iconic movies ever.

00:05:29.728 --> 00:05:31.697
It was great.
-You sort of --

00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:33.166
You and Rob Delaney,
the very funny Rob Delaney,

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.401
you sort of step into
the Harry and Marv role.

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:37.770
-Yeah, exactly.
-So sort of evil --

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.906
I should say ill befalls you.
-Yes.

00:05:40.906 --> 00:05:42.642
-This is -- what has happened
to you in this situation?

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:44.510
-Well, so it's not all
fun and games.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.079
-This is you as Aunt Dot.
-That's me as Aunt Dot.

00:05:47.079 --> 00:05:49.515
[ Laughter ]
-Aunt Dots.

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:51.450
-Very good.
Very good.

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.919
I'm Aunt --
I'm Aunt Dots there.

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:57.957
And I believe those were,
like, the marks where all my --

00:05:57.957 --> 00:05:59.825
I get sort of hit by Nerf darts,

00:05:59.825 --> 00:06:02.161
and I think that's where
all the marks were.

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:03.763
But I'm not happy.

00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:05.431
I mean, we had a lot of fun
making the movie.

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:07.834
But it wasn't always fun.
I was miserable that day.

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:09.435
-I would imagine.
-Yes.

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:11.871
-This is -- You don't
look happy here. Hold on.

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:13.372
This is not --
I've seen you look happy.

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:14.573
-I know.
-This is --

00:06:14.573 --> 00:06:16.675
[ Laughter ]
-You guys -- like, my mug shot.

00:06:16.675 --> 00:06:19.212
-This you when me and the dog
got on the elevator,

00:06:19.212 --> 00:06:21.080
it feels like.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.582
That was the face
you managed to get.

00:06:22.582 --> 00:06:26.285
-It's like, "Again? You're
really pushing this dog on me."

00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:28.254
Yeah.
-What about -- I did hear you

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:30.223
laughed harder during
the filming of one scene

00:06:30.223 --> 00:06:31.990
than you expected to.
-I know, yeah.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:34.060
This is a little disgusting --
not disgusting to me.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:37.162
I'm a -- You've seen a lot.
You have kids.

00:06:37.162 --> 00:06:39.765
I don't know what that means.
But I'm a mom.

00:06:39.765 --> 00:06:41.367
I've seen a lot, too.

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:43.569
I sometimes laugh
a little too hard.

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:46.105
And there's a scene in a pool,
which was so funny.

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:48.140
The pool was very warm,
it was very comfortable.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:49.708
It felt like I was just, like,
at a water park,

00:06:49.708 --> 00:06:53.012
and Rob was so -- it was funny.
I was sliding and slipping.

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:54.379
And I laughed so hard --

00:06:54.379 --> 00:06:55.514
This is the first time
I'm saying this --

00:06:55.514 --> 00:06:57.983
I wet my pants in the pool.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.983 --> 00:07:00.286
And I was too worried
to say anything because, like,

00:07:00.286 --> 00:07:02.855
health restrictions, like we'd
have to shut down production.

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:05.924
But it's over now!
I wet the pool!

00:07:05.924 --> 00:07:07.994
[ Laughter and applause ]
-Oh, my God.

00:07:07.994 --> 00:07:09.561
We just love it!

00:07:09.561 --> 00:07:11.596
I mean, when we can break

00:07:11.596 --> 00:07:14.133
Hollywood stories
on this show...

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:16.669
-That's your M.O.
-That is so important to us.

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:18.003
-I know it is!

00:07:18.003 --> 00:07:19.405
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:07:19.405 --> 00:07:20.939
It is always a delight
to see you.

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:23.442
Ellie Kemper.
"Home Sweet Home Alone."

00:07:23.442 --> 00:07:25.378
It's streaming now on Disney+.

